    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "29BB(2) How duty is imposed",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-29bb-ss-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.94",
      "values:originalText": "(2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law\nof the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:\n\n(a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State\nor Territory concerned; and\n\n(b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is\nconsistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the\nduties that may be imposed on the Council.\nNote:\nIf this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by\nforce of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having\nconsented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that\nlaw).\nCommonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but\nState or Territory legislative powers are not",
      "skos:definition": "(2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law\nof the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:\n\n(a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State\nor Territory concerned; and\n\n(b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is\nconsistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the\nduties that may be imposed on the Council.\nNote:\nIf this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by\nforce of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having\nconsented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that\nlaw).\nCommonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but\nState or Territory legislative powers are not",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "2359d61868019d8aaacb139bc6e2e4e4403c0623f2af4f815a5648053d2fa9e6",
      "skos:note": "Duty is not imposed by this Part to the extent it exceeds State legislative powers."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-29bb-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "29BB(2) How duty is imposed",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 94,
      "values:originalText": "(2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law\nof the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:\n\n(a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State\nor Territory concerned; and\n\n(b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is\nconsistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the\nduties that may be imposed on the Council.\nNote:\nIf this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by\nforce of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having\nconsented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that\nlaw).\nCommonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but\nState or Territory legislative powers are not"
    },
