      "cml:operator": "must",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Tribunal's decision required for declaration operation",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(2) If an application for review of a declaration is made within 21 days after the day the declaration is published; and (b) the Tribunal makes an order under section 44KA staying the operation of the declaration;",
      "cml:conclusionText": "the declaration does not begin to operate until the order is no longer in effect under subsection 44KA(6) or the Tribunal makes a new decision."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-44j",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "44J Revocation of declaration",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-44j"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.158",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Council may recommend to the designated Minister that a\ndeclaration be revoked. The Council must have regard to the\nobjects of this Part in making its decision.\n\n(2) The Council cannot recommend revocation of a declaration unless\nit is satisfied that, at the time of the recommendation:\n\n(a) subsection 44F(1) would prevent the making of an\napplication for a recommendation that the service concerned\nbe declared; or\n\n(b) subsection 44H(4) would prevent the service concerned from\nbeing declared.\n\n(3) On receiving a revocation recommendation, the designated\nMinister must either revoke the declaration or decide not to revoke\nthe declaration.\n\n(3A) The designated Minister must have regard to the objects of this\nPart in making his or her decision.\n\n(4) The designated Minister must publish the decision to revoke or not\nto revoke.\n\n(5) If the designated Minister decides not to revoke, the designated\nMinister must give reasons for the decision to the provider of the\n\nAccess to services  Chapter 2\nAccess to services  Part IIIA\nDeclared services  Division 2\n\nSection 44K\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\ndeclared service when the designated Minister publishes the\ndecision.\n\n(6) The designated Minister cannot revoke a declaration without\nreceiving a revocation recommendation.\n\n(7) If the designated Minister does not publish under subsection (4) his\nor her decision on the revocation recommendation within the\nperiod starting at the start of the day the recommendation is\nreceived and ending at the end of 60 days after that day, the\ndesignated Minister is taken, immediately after the end of that\n60-day period:\n\n(a) to have made a decision that the declaration be revoked; and\n\n(b) to have published that decision in accordance with this\nsection.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Council may recommend to the designated Minister that a\ndeclaration be revoked. The Council must have regard to the\nobjects of this Part in making its decision.\n\n(2) The Council cannot recommend revocation of a declaration unless\nit is satisfied that, at the time of the recommendation:\n\n(a) subsection 44F(1) would prevent the making of an\napplication for a recommendation that the service concerned\nbe declared; or\n\n(b) subsection 44H(4) would prevent the service concerned from\nbeing declared.\n\n(3) On receiving a revocation recommendation, the designated\nMinister must either revoke the declaration or decide not to revoke\nthe declaration.\n\n(3A) The designated Minister must have regard to the objects of this\nPart in making his or her decision.\n\n(4) The designated Minister must publish the decision to revoke or not\nto revoke.\n\n(5) If the designated Minister decides not to revoke, the designated\nMinister must give reasons for the decision to the provider of the\n\nAccess to services  Chapter 2\nAccess to services  Part IIIA\nDeclared services  Division 2\n\nSection 44K\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\ndeclared service when the designated Minister publishes the\ndecision.\n\n(6) The designated Minister cannot revoke a declaration without\nreceiving a revocation recommendation.\n\n(7) If the designated Minister does not publish under subsection (4) his\nor her decision on the revocation recommendation within the\nperiod starting at the start of the day the recommendation is\nreceived and ending at the end of 60 days after that day, the\ndesignated Minister is taken, immediately after the end of that\n60-day period:\n\n(a) to have made a decision that the declaration be revoked; and\n\n(b) to have published that decision in accordance with this\nsection.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-44j-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-44j-ss-2"
