      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "45AG Giving effect to a cartel provision",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-45ag"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.292",
      "values:originalText": "Offence\n\n(1) A corporation commits an offence if:\n\n(a) a contract, arrangement or understanding contains a cartel\nprovision; and\n\n(b) the corporation gives effect to the cartel provision.\nNote:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\n\n(2) The fault element for paragraph (1)(a) is knowledge or belief.\nPenalty\n\n(3) An offence against subsection (1) is punishable on conviction by a\nfine not exceeding the greater of the following:\n\n(a) $100,000,000;\n\n(b) if the court can determine the total value of the benefits that:\n\n(i) have been obtained by one or more persons; and\n\n(ii) are reasonably attributable to the commission of the\noffence;\n\n3 times that total value;\n\n(c) if the court cannot determine the total value of those\nbenefits—30% of the corporation’s adjusted turnover during\nthe breach turnover period for the offence.\n\nRestrictive trade practices  Chapter 3\nRestrictive trade practices  Part IV\nCartel conduct  Division 1\n\nSection 45AH\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nPre-commencement contracts etc.\n\n(4) Paragraph (1)(a) applies to contracts or arrangements made, or\nunderstandings arrived at, before, at or after the commencement of\nthis section.\nIndictable offence\n\n(5) An offence against subsection (1) is an indictable offence.",
      "skos:definition": "Offence\n\n(1) A corporation commits an offence if:\n\n(a) a contract, arrangement or understanding contains a cartel\nprovision; and\n\n(b) the corporation gives effect to the cartel provision.\nNote:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\n\n(2) The fault element for paragraph (1)(a) is knowledge or belief.\nPenalty\n\n(3) An offence against subsection (1) is punishable on conviction by a\nfine not exceeding the greater of the following:\n\n(a) $100,000,000;\n\n(b) if the court can determine the total value of the benefits that:\n\n(i) have been obtained by one or more persons; and\n\n(ii) are reasonably attributable to the commission of the\noffence;\n\n3 times that total value;\n\n(c) if the court cannot determine the total value of those\nbenefits—30% of the corporation’s adjusted turnover during\nthe breach turnover period for the offence.\n\nRestrictive trade practices  Chapter 3\nRestrictive trade practices  Part IV\nCartel conduct  Division 1\n\nSection 45AH\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nPre-commencement contracts etc.\n\n(4) Paragraph (1)(a) applies to contracts or arrangements made, or\nunderstandings arrived at, before, at or after the commencement of\nthis section.\nIndictable offence\n\n(5) An offence against subsection (1) is an indictable offence.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ag-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "cd25b8565d4b57e8f856cf50c696d80d49eb0c79087d2888c0de2bddefadce7e"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-45ag",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "45AG Giving effect to a cartel provision",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 292,
      "values:originalText": "Offence\n\n(1) A corporation commits an offence if:\n\n(a) a contract, arrangement or understanding contains a cartel\nprovision; and\n\n(b) the corporation gives effect to the cartel provision.\nNote:\nChapter 2 of the Criminal Code sets out the general principles of\ncriminal responsibility.\n\n(2) The fault element for paragraph (1)(a) is knowledge or belief.\nPenalty\n\n(3) An offence against subsection (1) is punishable on conviction by a\nfine not exceeding the greater of the following:\n\n(a) $100,000,000;\n\n(b) if the court can determine the total value of the benefits that:\n\n(i) have been obtained by one or more persons; and\n\n(ii) are reasonably attributable to the commission of the\noffence;\n\n3 times that total value;\n\n(c) if the court cannot determine the total value of those\nbenefits—30% of the corporation’s adjusted turnover during\nthe breach turnover period for the offence.\n\nRestrictive trade practices  Chapter 3\nRestrictive trade practices  Part IV\nCartel conduct  Division 1\n\nSection 45AH\n\nCompetition and Consumer Act 2010\n\nCompilation date: 27/05/2026\n\nPre-commencement contracts etc.\n\n(4) Paragraph (1)(a) applies to contracts or arrangements made, or\nunderstandings arrived at, before, at or after the commencement of\nthis section.\nIndictable offence\n\n(5) An offence against subsection (1) is an indictable offence."
    },
