    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ah",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "45AH Determining guilt",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-45ah"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.293",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A corporation may be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG even if:\n\n(a) each other party to the contract, arrangement or\nunderstanding is a person who is not criminally responsible;\nor\n\n(b) subject to subsection (2), all other parties to the contract,\narrangement or understanding have been acquitted of the\noffence.\nNote:\nParty has an extended meaning—see section 45AC.\n\n(2) A corporation cannot be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG if:\n\n(a) all other parties to the contract, arrangement or understanding\nhave been acquitted of such an offence; and\n\n(b) a finding of guilt would be inconsistent with their acquittal.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A corporation may be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG even if:\n\n(a) each other party to the contract, arrangement or\nunderstanding is a person who is not criminally responsible;\nor\n\n(b) subject to subsection (2), all other parties to the contract,\narrangement or understanding have been acquitted of the\noffence.\nNote:\nParty has an extended meaning—see section 45AC.\n\n(2) A corporation cannot be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG if:\n\n(a) all other parties to the contract, arrangement or understanding\nhave been acquitted of such an offence; and\n\n(b) a finding of guilt would be inconsistent with their acquittal.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ah-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-45ah-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "7a83b2d57870ad22e66ccfcf8c253e01e0f76740870ce4a52c19c608eda0885b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-act-2010#sec-45ah",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "45AH Determining guilt",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 293,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A corporation may be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG even if:\n\n(a) each other party to the contract, arrangement or\nunderstanding is a person who is not criminally responsible;\nor\n\n(b) subject to subsection (2), all other parties to the contract,\narrangement or understanding have been acquitted of the\noffence.\nNote:\nParty has an extended meaning—see section 45AC.\n\n(2) A corporation cannot be found guilty of an offence against\nsection 45AF or 45AG if:\n\n(a) all other parties to the contract, arrangement or understanding\nhave been acquitted of such an offence; and\n\n(b) a finding of guilt would be inconsistent with their acquittal."
    },
